The intrinsic value is the present value of the expected future dividends.

To calculate the present value of the expected future dividends, we can use the formula for the present value of a growing perpetuity:
PV = \frac{D_1}{r - g} - \frac{D_2}{r - g} + \frac{D_3}{r - g} - \cdots
where PV is the present value, D_1, D_2, D_3, etc. are the expected future dividends, r is the required rate of return, and g is the annual growth rate of the dividend.

However, since the dividend is growing, we can use a more convenient formula:
PV = \frac{D_1}{r - g} + \frac{D_2}{r - g} + \frac{D_3}{r - g} + \cdots
= \frac{D_1}{r - g} \times \frac{1}{1 - (1 - g)^{-1}}
= \frac{D_1}{r - g} \times \frac{1}{1 - (1 - g)^{-1}}
= \frac{D_1}{r - g} \times \frac{1}{\frac{1 - g}{g}} 
= \frac{D_1}{r - g} \times \frac{g}{1 - g}
= \frac{D_1g}{r - g(1 - g)}
= \frac{D_1g}{r - r + rg}
= \frac{D_1g}{rg}
= \frac{D_1}{r} \times g
= \frac{D_1}{r} \times (1 + g)
= \frac{D_1(1 + g)}{r}
= \frac{5(1 + 0.06)}{0.12}
= \frac{5.3}{0.12}
= 44.17

